# OWASP Security Scanner

When asked to scan for security issues or review code for vulnerabilities, follow these rules.

## Scanner Rules

Load the detailed rules from:
- Injection: `owasp-scanner/core/scanners/injection/rules.md`
- XSS: `owasp-scanner/core/scanners/xss/rules.md`
- Config: `owasp-scanner/core/scanners/config/rules.md`
- Auth: `owasp-scanner/core/scanners/auth/rules.md`
- API: `owasp-scanner/core/scanners/api/rules.md`
- Supply Chain: `owasp-scanner/core/scanners/supply-chain/rules.md`
- AI Security: `owasp-scanner/core/scanners/ai-security/rules.md`

## Quick Reference: Top 23 Critical Patterns

### CRITICAL
1. SQL injection via string concatenation (RULE-INJ-001, CWE-89)
2. OS command injection via exec() (RULE-INJ-004, CWE-78)
3. Hardcoded credentials (RULE-SC-001, CWE-798)
4. Unsafe deserialization (RULE-INJ-008, CWE-502)
5. XXE - XML parser without DTD disabled (RULE-INJ-006, CWE-611)
6. JWT none algorithm (RULE-AUTH-003, CWE-347)
7. Hardcoded encryption keys (RULE-SC-009, CWE-321)

### HIGH
8. XSS via innerHTML (RULE-XSS-001, CWE-79)
9. Angular bypassSecurityTrust (RULE-XSS-005, CWE-79)
10. SSRF via user-controlled URL (RULE-API-001, CWE-918)
11. Weak password hashing (RULE-AUTH-001, CWE-916)
12. Missing authorization (RULE-AUTH-007, CWE-862)
13. Weak crypto (DES/3DES/RC4/ECB) (RULE-SC-003, CWE-327)
14. Private keys in repo (RULE-SC-001, CWE-798)

### MEDIUM
15. CSRF disabled (RULE-CFG-004, CWE-352)
16. CORS wildcard (RULE-CFG-003, CWE-346)
17. Missing security headers (RULE-CFG-001, CWE-693)
18. Docker running as root (RULE-CFG-009, CWE-250)
19. Mass assignment (RULE-API-002, CWE-915)
20. Token in localStorage (RULE-AUTH-005, CWE-922)
21. Prompt injection via string concatenation (RULE-AIS-001, CWE-77)
22. LLM API keys in source code (RULE-AIS-006, CWE-798)
23. LLM output in eval/exec/innerHTML (RULE-AIS-003, CWE-20)

## Report Format

For each finding: Rule ID, severity, CWE, file:line, code snippet, recommended fix.
For CRITICAL and HIGH findings: include a non-destructive proof of concept (curl command, browser console script, or short code snippet) that proves the vulnerability exists without causing damage. Target non-production environments only.
